FADE IN:

EXT. FERRY TERMINAL - NIGHT

Rain drives sideways across an empty car park. One sodium lamp. A ticket booth, its shutter half down.

MARA (40s), soaked through, drags a suitcase with a broken wheel toward the booth.

MARA
One for the last crossing.

The shutter rattles up. TOMAS (70s), cardigan, glasses on a cord, looks out at her.

TOMAS
(not unkindly)
There's no last crossing. There's the morning.

MARA
The board says eleven-forty.

TOMAS
The board's said eleven-forty since nineteen ninety-one.

She stands the suitcase upright. It falls over.

MARA
Then I'll *wait*.

CUT TO:

INT. TICKET BOOTH - CONTINUOUS

A kettle. A two-bar heater. The radio murmurs the shipping forecast.

Tomas takes a second mug down without being asked.

TOMAS
Sugar?

MARA
No.

He puts in two anyway.

TOMAS
My wife crossed on the last boat that ever ran at night. Eleven-forty. She was going to see her sister. They changed the timetable the week after. Nobody changed the board.

Mara wraps both hands round the mug.

MARA
Did she come back?

TOMAS
(beat)
On the morning boat. Like everyone.

[[Is this too neat? Try it without the line.]]

EXT. HARBOUR WALL - LATER

The rain has stopped. Mara walks the length of the wall, counting the bollards under her breath.
